1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,719
Your branch is ready.

2
00:00:01,719 --> 00:00:03,924
But you cannot just push it into main,

3
00:00:03,924 --> 00:00:06,303
because main is what everyone depends on.

4
00:00:06,853 --> 00:00:10,696
Today you use a pull request: a proposal to merge your branch,

5
00:00:10,696 --> 00:00:14,106
with a conversation and a review before anything lands.

6
00:00:14,656 --> 00:00:19,328
You also protect main, so nobody can skip that review, not even by accident.

7
00:00:19,878 --> 00:00:23,931
And you watch all of it live, on a real Git server running on this computer.

8
00:00:25,481 --> 00:00:28,089
A pull request is a page about one branch.

9
00:00:28,089 --> 00:00:31,256
It says: I want to merge this branch into that one.

10
00:00:31,256 --> 00:00:32,436
Please take a look.

11
00:00:32,986 --> 00:00:34,415
It shows the commits,

12
00:00:34,415 --> 00:00:35,640
the changed lines,

13
00:00:35,640 --> 00:00:36,934
and a conversation.

14
00:00:36,934 --> 00:00:39,044
GitHub calls it a pull request.

15
00:00:39,044 --> 00:00:41,222
GitLab calls it a merge request.

16
00:00:41,222 --> 00:00:41,903
Same idea.

17
00:00:42,453 --> 00:00:46,976
A review has three outcomes. Comment. Approve. Or request changes.

18
00:00:47,526 --> 00:00:50,308
A protected branch is a rule on the server.

19
00:00:50,308 --> 00:00:52,766
For example: no direct pushes to main,

20
00:00:52,766 --> 00:00:55,419
and at least one approval before a merge.

21
00:00:55,969 --> 00:00:57,302
Our demo uses Gitea,

22
00:00:57,302 --> 00:01:01,701
a free Git server with the same words and almost the same screens.

23
00:01:01,701 --> 00:01:02,367
On GitHub,

24
00:01:02,367 --> 00:01:04,367
the rule lives under Settings,

25
00:01:04,367 --> 00:01:05,300
then Branches.

26
00:01:05,300 --> 00:01:07,233
We point out each difference.

27
00:01:08,783 --> 00:01:11,962
Sam opens the team app repository, and signs in.

28
00:01:19,712 --> 00:01:21,973
First, protect main. Click Settings.

29
00:01:22,523 --> 00:01:24,464
Then Branches, and Add New Rule.

30
00:01:27,614 --> 00:01:29,876
The pattern is the branch name: main.

31
00:01:30,426 --> 00:01:34,394
Scroll down. Push is disabled: nobody can push straight to main.

32
00:01:34,944 --> 00:01:37,333
Now require one approval before a merge.

33
00:01:38,283 --> 00:01:40,459
Save the rule. Main is protected.

34
00:01:45,209 --> 00:01:47,214
Now the work. Click Pull Requests.

35
00:01:48,464 --> 00:01:52,304
New Pull Request. Merge into main. Pull from the feature branch.

36
00:01:57,554 --> 00:02:01,565
Below, Git shows exactly what would change: two commits, two files.

37
00:02:02,115 --> 00:02:06,958
Press New Pull Request. Give it a clear title, and a short description: what, and why.

38
00:02:11,808 --> 00:02:13,173
Create Pull Request.

39
00:02:16,523 --> 00:02:21,088
This is the pull request page. Conversation, Commits, and Files Changed.

40
00:02:23,638 --> 00:02:25,234
And look at the merge box.

41
00:02:25,234 --> 00:02:29,283
This pull request does not have enough approvals yet: zero of one.

42
00:02:29,283 --> 00:02:30,572
The protection works.

43
00:02:32,122 --> 00:02:37,007
Now Alex, a teammate, opens the pull request on his own computer, and signs in.

44
00:02:41,557 --> 00:02:45,099
He opens Files Changed, and reads the difference like a reviewer.

45
00:02:45,649 --> 00:02:49,659
Hover over a line, click the plus, and comment on exactly that line.

46
00:02:53,209 --> 00:02:57,583
Start review, so your comments are sent together. Then open the Review menu.

47
00:03:01,433 --> 00:03:03,737
Request changes, with a short summary.

48
00:03:08,987 --> 00:03:09,757
Back to Sam.

49
00:03:09,757 --> 00:03:12,132
He fixes the code on his own machine,

50
00:03:12,132 --> 00:03:12,645
commits,

51
00:03:12,645 --> 00:03:14,571
and pushes to the same branch.

52
00:03:14,571 --> 00:03:16,496
No new pull request is needed.

53
00:03:17,046 --> 00:03:20,715
The pull request updates by itself: one new commit appears.

54
00:03:21,265 --> 00:03:25,127
Alex reads the change again, opens the Review menu, and approves.

55
00:03:32,477 --> 00:03:35,421
Now the merge box turns blue. It can be merged.

56
00:03:38,471 --> 00:03:42,204
Click the arrow next to the merge button. There are several ways to merge.

57
00:03:42,754 --> 00:03:46,027
A merge commit keeps every commit and adds one merge.

58
00:03:46,027 --> 00:03:47,818
Rebase keeps a straight line.

59
00:03:47,818 --> 00:03:50,968
Squash turns the whole branch into a single commit.

60
00:03:51,518 --> 00:03:54,995
A team picks one rule. We choose squash, for a tidy main.

61
00:03:55,545 --> 00:03:57,428
The message is filled in for you.

62
00:03:57,428 --> 00:03:58,513
Tick Delete Branch,

63
00:03:58,513 --> 00:04:00,510
because the work will live on main,

64
00:04:00,510 --> 00:04:02,222
then confirm the squash merge.

65
00:04:08,372 --> 00:04:11,764
Merged. The pull request is closed, and the branch is gone.

66
00:04:12,314 --> 00:04:16,645
Look at main: one clean commit, with the pull request number in its message.

67
00:04:18,195 --> 00:04:21,203
Your task, on your own GitHub account. It is free.

68
00:04:21,753 --> 00:04:24,477
The checker is lab sixty-two dot S H,

69
00:04:24,477 --> 00:04:28,231
in the academy slash lab folder of git hub dot com,

70
00:04:28,231 --> 00:04:29,703
slash it dash waves,

71
00:04:29,703 --> 00:04:31,396
slash dev ops dash A I.

72
00:04:31,946 --> 00:04:35,007
One: create a public repository called team app,

73
00:04:35,007 --> 00:04:35,900
with one file,

74
00:04:35,900 --> 00:04:38,324
and push a branch with a small change.

75
00:04:38,874 --> 00:04:43,632
Two: open a pull request from that branch into main, and leave one review comment.

76
00:04:44,182 --> 00:04:49,046
Three: protect main, under Settings, Branches, so a pull request is required.

77
00:04:49,596 --> 00:04:52,476
Four: merge the pull request, and delete the branch.

78
00:04:53,026 --> 00:04:55,652
Then run: sh lab sixty-two dot S H,

79
00:04:55,652 --> 00:04:56,102
check,

80
00:04:56,102 --> 00:04:57,228
your user name,

81
00:04:57,228 --> 00:04:57,678
slash,

82
00:04:57,678 --> 00:04:58,353
team app.

83
00:04:58,353 --> 00:04:59,554
Four pass lines.

84
00:05:01,104 --> 00:05:03,088
Six points, and you need all six.

85
00:05:03,638 --> 00:05:05,473
The checker shows four pass lines.

86
00:05:06,023 --> 00:05:09,116
Main is protected, and you did not push to it directly.

87
00:05:09,666 --> 00:05:12,610
Your pull request has a clear title and description.

88
00:05:13,160 --> 00:05:16,147
You can explain comment, approve and request changes.

89
00:05:16,697 --> 00:05:18,787
The branch is deleted after the merge.

90
00:05:19,337 --> 00:05:21,300
You can say why a team protects main.

91
00:05:21,850 --> 00:05:23,471
To pass, you need all six.

92
00:05:25,021 --> 00:05:27,261
Your turn, on your own GitHub account.

93
00:05:27,811 --> 00:05:30,520
One: create the repository and push a branch.

94
00:05:31,070 --> 00:05:33,481
Two: open the pull request, and review it.

95
00:05:34,031 --> 00:05:35,396
Three: protect main.

96
00:05:35,946 --> 00:05:37,845
Four: merge and delete the branch.

97
00:05:38,395 --> 00:05:39,846
Five: run lab check.

98
00:05:40,396 --> 00:05:44,150
Six: delete the practice repository when you no longer need it.

99
00:05:44,700 --> 00:05:46,535
Pause the video, and begin now.

100
00:05:48,085 --> 00:05:48,960
Quick check.

101
00:05:49,510 --> 00:05:50,832
What is a pull request?

102
00:05:51,382 --> 00:05:53,110
What are the three review outcomes?

103
00:05:53,660 --> 00:05:55,388
What does a protected branch stop?

104
00:05:55,938 --> 00:05:57,858
Why delete the branch after the merge?

105
00:05:58,408 --> 00:06:00,943
Objective covered: M C zero seven,

106
00:06:00,943 --> 00:06:01,987
objective six,

107
00:06:01,987 --> 00:06:03,031
pull requests,

108
00:06:03,031 --> 00:06:03,627
reviews,

109
00:06:03,627 --> 00:06:05,342
and protected branches.

110
00:06:05,892 --> 00:06:07,961
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

